{
  "evaluation": "C best applies the explicit apparent-conflict rule: Study A measures a depression dimension within a quality-of-life instrument, whereas Study B uses a dedicated depression scale. It also correctly notes that Study A's stated improvement contradicts its extracted harm label. B reaches the same appropriate verdict and reason, but emphasizes scale polarity more than the rule's distinction between what was measured. A notices the mislabeled improvement, but incorrectly assigns design_or_bias: an uncontrolled case series versus an RCT does not itself dissolve a conflict, and no qualifying underpowering signal is provided.",
  "ranking": ["C", "B", "A"]
}
